-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Gradient Amplitude:** During early training, mi-MLP ensures deeper layers receive large gradients while shallower layers receive extremely small ones, unlike vanilla MLP where gradients are similar across layers. This behavior is critical for convergence [S1].
-*   **Weight Initialization:** The condition $\|\mathbf{w}_i^{j1}\|_1 \in (0, 1]$ is theoretically required for the gradient ratio property. Default PyTorch initialization satisfies this, but custom initialization must respect this bound [S1].
-*   **Separate Modeling:** While mi-MLP defines the layer structure, the paper recommends modeling density and color separately to reduce artifacts, as geometry is smoother than appearance. Using a single shared head may leave residual artifacts [S1].
+## Subtopics
+
+### Separate Density and Color Modeling
+A companion technique often used with mi-MLP where density and color are predicted by separate MLPs or branches, allowing different positional encoding frequencies for geometry and appearance [S1].
+
+### Regularization Terms
+Additional regularization terms proposed in the source to further reduce artifacts when using mi-MLP [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Similar to vanilla NeRF per forward pass, but potentially faster convergence in few-shot settings due to alleviated overfitting.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Slightly higher memory usage per layer due to the concatenation of input embeddings $\gamma_L(\mathbf{x})$ at every layer, increasing the input dimension to each linear layer from $d_{prev}$ to $d_{prev} + d_{input}$ [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Boosts PSNR significantly in few-shot view synthesis (e.g., from 14.73 to 24.23 on specific benchmarks) compared to baseline NeRF [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Prevents overfitting in data-scarce scenarios; maintains high-frequency detail synthesis; plug-and-play with existing NeRF pipelines [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful handling of input encoding dimensions; may introduce artifacts if density and color are not modeled separately [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Similar to standard NeRF per forward pass, but slightly higher due to the concatenation of encoded inputs at every layer. The number of parameters is comparable to the baseline if the hidden dimension is kept constant, as the input dimension to each layer increases by $d_{enc}$ [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Slightly higher memory usage during training due to the larger input dimension to each linear layer, but no significant change in inference memory compared to standard NeRF [S1].
+*   **Performance:** The method boosts PSNR from 14.73 to 24.23 on few-shot view synthesis benchmarks compared to the baseline [S1]. It achieves state-of-the-art results on a wide range of benchmarks [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Prevents overfitting in few-shot scenarios without reducing model capacity; plug-and-play solution; maintains high-frequency detail synthesis [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful selection of positional encoding frequencies; relies on specific weight initialization properties for optimal gradient behavior [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Few-shot view synthesis where standard NeRF overfits; when model capacity needs to be preserved but overfitting is a concern [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When training on large datasets with many views, where standard NeRF may not overfit and the added complexity is unnecessary [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The mi-MLP was introduced in the paper "Is Vanilla MLP in Neural Radiance Field Enough for Few-Shot View Synthesis?" (CVPR 2024) [S1]. It addresses the observation that trivially reducing parameters alleviates overfitting but loses detail. By incorporating inputs into each layer, it provides an orthogonal solution to the overfitting problem in few-shot view synthesis, revealing that shorter paths between inputs and outputs allow for effective synthesis with fewer parameters while keeping model capacity unchanged [S1].
+The multi-input MLP (mi-MLP) was proposed in the paper "Is Vanilla MLP in Neural Radiance Field Enough for Few-Shot View Synthesis?" [S1]. The authors observed that trivially reducing model parameters alleviates overfitting but harms detail synthesis. They introduced mi-MLP as an orthogonal method from the perspective of network structure, incorporating inputs into each layer to prevent overfitting without harming detailed synthesis [S1]. The method was shown to significantly improve PSNR on few-shot view synthesis tasks [S1].
 
